---
// Page shell for /tools/stock-picking: intro + the StockPickingModule island
// (how many stocks, superstocks) + sources and next-tool link.
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import StockPickingModule from "../../components/StockPickingModule.tsx";
import NextTool from "../../components/NextTool.astro";
import Sources from "../../components/Sources.astro";
import "../../styles/simtool.css";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Stock-Picking: How Many, and Why a Few Win"
  description="Two hard truths about owning individual stocks: diversifying within stocks cuts risk but only to a floor, and most stocks actually lose to Treasury bills while a tiny few create all the market's wealth."
>
  <section class="container wl-intro">
    <p class="eyebrow">Interactive Tool</p>
    <h1>The trouble with picking stocks</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Two findings, from real stock-level data, that together make the case against
      hand-picking a few names. First: adding stocks cuts risk, but only down to a
      floor you can't diversify away. Second, and more startling: most individual
      stocks actually <em>underperform</em> Treasury bills over their lifetimes,
      while a tiny handful create essentially all the market's wealth. Miss those,
      and you miss everything.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="container wl-host">
    <StockPickingModule client:load />
  </section>

  <section class="container wl-explainer">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>How many stocks is enough?</h2>
      <p>
        Holding one stock is a gamble on one company; holding many averages the
        company-specific luck away. But the benefit fades fast: most of the
        diversification is captured in the first few dozen names, and no amount of
        adding removes the <strong>market</strong> risk they all share. You hit a
        floor: the undiversifiable risk you're actually paid to bear.
      </p>
      <h2>Why owning them all beats picking a few</h2>
      <p>
        Here's the catch that floor hides. Stock returns are wildly <strong>skewed</strong>:
        across decades, most stocks lose to T-bills, and a small percentage account
        for the entire equity risk premium. That makes stock-picking a game of trying
        to catch a few needles in a giant haystack, and missing them is the common
        case. Owning the whole haystack guarantees you hold the winners. It's the
        deepest argument for a broad index fund. Educational only, not financial advice.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <Sources tool="stock-picking" />

  <NextTool current="/tools/stock-picking" />
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .wl-intro {
    padding-block: var(--space-lg) var(--space-md);
    max-width: var(--measure-wide);
  }
  .lede {
    font-size: var(--step-1);
    color: var(--color-text-soft);
    line-height: 1.5;
  }
  .wl-explainer {
    margin-top: var(--space-lg);
  }
</style>
